# 1. Method and graph semantics

## 1.1 Source and evidence discipline

Nodes are derived from Capability & Ownership Map v1.5 capability rows whose posture is `DEFINED — DESIGN REQUIRED`, `DEFINED — UNIMPLEMENTED`, `IMPLEMENTED — EXTEND`, or an execution-only unimplemented capability where it materially affects dependency reasoning.

Implemented-status claims are refreshed against the published M14 baseline where M11–M14 directly closed the capability. This graph does not promote an architecture principle into an `IMPLEMENTED` capability merely because the principle is already binding.

Every material dependency is grounded in at least one of:

```text
Architecture Pattern Register v2.4
Product & Production Architecture v1.6
Capability & Ownership Map v1.5
accepted full-sequence pressure-test result
```

Edges are never justified only by implementation convenience.

## 1.2 Dependency strength

### HARD

```text
A ──HARD──▶ B
```

B cannot be implemented and source-gated correctly in the declared scope until A exists.

### CONDITIONAL

```text
A ──CONDITIONAL──▶ B
```

B does not require A in every case, but any B path that claims or consumes A's domain must satisfy the integration contract.

### SURFACE

```text
A ──SURFACE──▶ UI
```

The user surface depends on A but owns no new production truth.

## 1.3 Dependency reason

Strength and reason are orthogonal.

| Reason | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **IDENTITY** | The consumer is defined in terms of an upstream durable identity. |
| **CONTRACT** | A design/validation contract must exist before the consumer can be completed safely. |
| **CO-DESIGN** | Two capabilities must freeze coherently; the HARD projection uses one direction only to keep the graph acyclic. |
| **HISTORICAL-MIRROR** | Historical/read-side support must land with or after the capture-side extension it mirrors. |

`INDEPENDENT` is an explicit audit assertion, not an edge.

## 1.4 Dependency class

| Class | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **AUTHORITY** | The dependency constrains identity, production truth, adoption, publication, readiness, or immutable capture. |
| **EXECUTION** | The dependency is downstream of captured authority: observation, materialization, QC evidence, or executor behavior. |

A coordination or execution capability may own an operation or artifact without becoming a production-truth authority.

## 1.5 Co-design without graph cycles

A co-design pair is not represented as two opposing HARD arrows. Instead:

```text
one directional HARD edge
+
CO-DESIGN reason / shared design gate
```

The directional edge represents prerequisite flow for mechanical validation; the gate requires the two contracts to freeze together where architecture demands it.

## 1.6 Implemented foundations

```text
BASE-SEMANTIC
    semantic identity, narrative topology, canonical ordering

BASE-CONTINUITY
    boundary transitions, random-access effective state, coherent current resolution

BASE-VISUAL
    approved visual identity and capture

BASE-SPATIAL
    SpatialWorld revisions, spatial tracks, Shot camera/staging plans,
    deterministic spatial realization inputs

BASE-CAPTURE
    immutable ShotRevision capture and readiness framework

BASE-EXECUTION
    durable Generation lifecycle, deterministic materialization seams,
    output import, Take candidacy

BASE-TAKE-SELECT
    Take approval/rejection, explicit approved/select boundary, Shot canon pointer

BASE-HISTORY
    Exact Rerun isolation, fail-closed provenance, recovery, bounded-query proofs
```

These are foundations, not graph gaps.

M11–M14 also closed several nodes that were gaps in v1.3. They remain visible below as **IMPLEMENTED / CLOSED** nodes so successor dependencies and historical cut points remain mechanically reviewable; v1.4 does not delete a node merely because its prerequisite was implemented.

